[[Category:Olympiad Combinatorics Problems]]

Proof by contradiction:

Suppose <imath>p^2=2d-1</imath>, <imath>q^2=5d-1</imath>, <imath>r^2=13d-1</imath>, for integers <imath>p, q, r</imath>. Using the first and third equation, we get <imath>60p^2=130d-60</imath> and <imath>10r^2=130d-10</imath>. Subtracting these two equations, we get <imath>10r^2-60p^2=-10-60</imath>, giving <imath>r^2 - 6p^2=5</imath>, or <imath>r^2 = 6p^2 + 5</imath>. However, the RHS is equal to 2 mod 3. Perfect squares can never be 2 mod 3, so no such r exists. We are done.

